WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Data sensitivity should shape temporary-file design.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:15.000
Temporary files may contain secrets, personal data, generated reports, intermediate transformed data, or content that is safe only after a later redaction or validation step.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.620
The program should decide whether that data belongs on disk at all.

4
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.380
Temporary does not always mean private.

5
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.160
A file may be readable by another account because of directory or mode settings.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.260
It may remain after a crash.

7
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.200
It may be included in backups, snapshots, diagnostics, or operational collection.

8
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.660
It may be visible through logs or monitoring tools that record paths and names.

9
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.740
Avoid storing sensitive values unless the workflow truly requires it.

10
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:54.800
If data can remain in memory, stream through a pipe, or be represented by a handle without writing sensitive content to disk, that may reduce persistence and exposure.

11
00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:00.900
When disk storage is necessary, permissions and cleanup need to match the sensitivity.

12
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.700
Intermediate content can be more sensitive than final output.

13
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:13.200
Decrypted data, unredacted reports, raw imports, temporary exports, and expanded configuration values may reveal more than the final artifact.

14
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.740
Reviewers should ask what the file contains during every phase, not only what it becomes at the end.

15
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.780
Diagnostics should avoid exposing sensitive paths, names, or values unnecessarily.

16
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:32.960
Logs can identify a field, operation, failure category, or sanitized path without dumping secrets or untrusted content.

17
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.660
Redaction rules should be part of the temporary-file contract.

18
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.060
Retention expectations should be clear.

19
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:47.280
Is the file removed immediately, retained for troubleshooting, kept until a process exits, or cleaned by an external job?

20
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.740
The answer affects privacy, storage, compliance, and incident response.
